## A page that listens

A browser constantly notices what a visitor does: pressed, swiped, typed a letter, switched tabs, turned the phone. The browser wraps each observation in an **event** and tells the page. The page does nothing until it is “subscribed” to an event, and a script has to do the subscribing.

```js
const button = document.querySelector('#menu-button');

button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log('Clicked, the type of event:', event.type);
});
```

The `addEventListener` method takes two arguments: the **type of event** and a **handler**, the function the browser calls when the event happens. It passes the function an event object. The object has `type` (which event), `target` (where it happened), `key` for the keyboard (which key), and much else. A subscription can be removed with `removeEventListener`.

There are two older ways, and both are worse. An attribute in the markup (`<button onclick="...">`) mixes markup with code and allows only one handler. A property (`button.onclick = ...`) also gives one handler per event: the next one overwrites the last. `addEventListener` lets you subscribe as many functions to one event as you like.

## The events a site needs

There are dozens of events, but almost everything on a site is built on five:

- `click`: a press with a mouse or a finger, and on a button also the Enter and Space keys;
- `input`: the value of a field changes while the person types (while `change` fires when they have finished, for example left the field or switched a checkbox);
- `submit`: a form is sent, by the button or by Enter;
- `keydown`: a key is pressed, and `event.key` names it (`'Escape'`, `'Enter'`, `'a'`);
- `DOMContentLoaded`: the page is parsed. In our editor module scripts run after that moment anyway, so you don’t need to listen for it.

## Canceling the browser’s action

Many events have a **default** action: a link follows its address, a form is sent and the page reloads, Tab moves the focus. When you need to do it your own way, you cancel the action by calling `event.preventDefault()`.

```js
form.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();   // the page won't reload
  // then we send the data ourselves: that is the lesson on fetch
});
```

Without that line, Enter in the search field sends the form and the page reloads with an empty result: the most common beginner mistake.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what happens if you forget preventDefault in a submit handler?</summary>

The browser does the default action: it sends the form and reloads the page, and everything the script managed to do is gone.

</details>

## Bubbling and delegation

An event doesn’t stay in one place. When you click a link inside a card, the browser first gives the event to the link itself, and then it **bubbles** up the ancestors: the card, the container of the list, `body`, `document`. Each of them may have its own handler and receive the same event. That is why the event object has two addresses:

- `event.target`: where the event **happened** (the deepest element that was clicked);
- `event.currentTarget`: the element the **running** handler is attached to.

This gives a trick that everyone uses: **delegation**. Instead of a handler on each of a hundred cards, you attach one to the container and look at `event.target` to see what was actually clicked:

```js
cards.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  const card = event.target.closest('.card');   // the nearest card, going up from the click
  if (!card) return;                            // the click missed every card
  card.classList.toggle('selected');
});
```

The `closest` method looks for a matching element from the place of the click upward through the tree. There are two gains. There are fewer listeners, and above all the handler also works for cards that a script adds later: their events bubble up to the container too. So if you have a list that changes, attach the handler to the parent.

Bubbling can be stopped with `event.stopPropagation()`, but it is rarely worth doing: it breaks other people’s handlers that you may not have thought of.

See how an event travels the tree. Choose where to click, which nodes have handlers, and try to stop the bubbling:

<EventFlight />

And the second question of the diagram: how many handlers you need when the cards multiply and when a script has added one:

<EventFlight mode="delegate" />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: how does event.target differ from event.currentTarget?</summary>

`target` is where the event happened. `currentTarget` is the element the running handler is attached to. With delegation the handler is on the container (`currentTarget`), and an element inside it was clicked (`target`).

</details>

## Usable from the keyboard

The sneakiest mistake with events is to make something that works only with a mouse. Many people use the keyboard: people with motor impairments, people who listen to a page with a screen reader, and plain people who are faster without a mouse. The rules are short.

1. **Use a real `button`.** It gets focus from the Tab key, it is pressed with Enter and Space, a screen reader announces it as “button”, and all of that is free. A `div` with a `click` handler can do none of it, and a link `<a href="#">` that works as a button confuses both groups.
2. **Report the state.** A button that expands a block has an `aria-expanded` attribute (`false` or `true`), and the script changes it together with the block. The `aria-controls` attribute names the id of the controlled block. A screen reader will read “Sections, button, collapsed”.
3. **Close on Escape and return the focus.** If the menu was opened with a key, the focus stayed on the button. When you close the menu, don’t lose it: `button.focus()`.
4. **Don’t remove the visible focus.** The frame around the element that Tab has reached is the only way to know where you are.

The first rule has a second half: if the menu has to work without a script (and we’ll see why at the end of the part), then the list of sections is always visible in the markup, and the script hides it behind the button. Not the other way round.

## Practice

We continue with the 2014 page. We’ll add two typical elements: a menu that folds behind a button, and a search through the offers that hides the cards that don’t fit as you type.

Notice three details in the task. The `hidden` attribute loses to any CSS rule that sets `display`, so you need the rule `[hidden] { display: none !important }`. The search form gets a `submit` handler with `preventDefault`, or Enter will reload the page. And the cards are searched for at the moment of typing, not once at start: the list may change, and you’ll see that in the lesson on fetch.

As in the earlier lessons, the checks read the markup and the text of the script. Whether it works you check yourself: press Tab until you reach the menu button, open and close it with the keys, and type a word in the search.

## What was wrong with the old events

Before `addEventListener`, handlers were attached in different ways: Internet Explorer up to version 9 used `attachEvent`, the other browsers used `addEventListener`, and every developer wrote a wrapper of their own (jQuery, with its `.click()` and `.on()`, was among other things such a wrapper). Menus were made of links `<a href="#">` with “nothing” where the address should be: they could be clicked with a mouse, were not announced as buttons, and added a stray hash sign to the address bar. Bubbling was often stopped out of habit, and so other people’s scripts (analytics, for one) stopped seeing the clicks.

What stayed right: listen for events on a shared parent instead of attaching a hundred handlers, and treat the keyboard as equal to the mouse.
